{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"38d3ee1e446290b1","type":"group","x":2940,"y":-100,"width":2260,"height":1380,"label":"Survey Suggestions"},
		{
			"id":"22fa73c4e8560e11",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Project ideas\n1. Predict rating of a new item based on the user's past behavior \n2. next item prediction\n3. top-n recommendations",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-260,
			"y":-100,
			"width":540,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"0cf44b70fe59e17a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Data\n- explicit (direct user ratings or opinions) - ratings/likes/reviews/clear indication of preference - sparse and biased towards engaged users \n- implicit feedback (inferred from user behaviour) - clicks/dwell time/purchase - preferred due to low user effort \n\n- techniques - negative sampling \n\t- temporal decay (recent signals weighted more) \n\t- propensity weighting to handle bias\n\nNote: Implicit feedback represents noisy, biased proxies of the user preference",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-260,
			"y":100,
			"width":540,
			"height":360
		},
		{
			"id":"1f7c0b2e537a4e44",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Pitfalls\n- exposure bias (clicks depend on what was shown) \n- position bias (higher ranked items get more clicks) \n- confounding (promotions or UI changes after signals) \n- missing negatives (no clicks != dislike) \n- evaluation (offline metrics should align with online goals - A/B testing)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-260,
			"y":480,
			"width":540,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"6c123db44e409193",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Cold Start Problem\nnew user/new movie/new environment - recent events/popularity/user preference forms",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-260,
			"y":740,
			"width":540,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"ca80f205dea008d5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Additional Notes\n- data contract \n- agreement between data producer and consumer user/entity id\n- stable identity metrics timestamp semantics\n- event time (to be preferred for behaviour alignment)/log time\n- prevent future data leakage during training event types and schemas\n- impression/view/click/pay/add to cart/purchase/conversion\n- maintain consistency, reproducibility and reliability",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-260,
			"y":900,
			"width":540,
			"height":290
		},
		{
			"id":"0fbe74176a2455fe",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Evaluation Metrics\n- offline eval - based on historical data focus on top k metrics \n- evaluate per user and average across all users \n- sample metrics - precision@K/recall@K/NDCG@K/MAP@K",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-260,
			"y":1220,
			"width":540,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"ceb3049a898a3056",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Evaluation Splits\nrandom vs temporal splits (variants incl.) - prevent leakage (max train time < min test time)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-260,
			"y":1400,
			"width":540,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"c6b685946bb85cd4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Data Leakage Traps\n- label leakage \n- feature leakage \n- timestamp mistakes \n- cross-contamination\n- eval on warmed up state",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-260,
			"y":1540,
			"width":540,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"612ec7669bc6d626",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Baseline Recommenders\n- popularity based\n- recency based\n- co visitation based",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-260,
			"y":1780,
			"width":540,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"690b016e6ed18817",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"### Top-N recommendation",
			"styleAttributes":{"textAlign":"center"},
			"x":320,
			"y":-60,
			"width":440,
			"height":100,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"684ed8c55459a25c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"- Directly matches **implicit feedback**\n    \n- Naturally incorporates:\n    \n    - Negative sampling\n        \n    - Temporal decay\n        \n    - Propensity weighting\n        \n- Evaluation metrics already aligned (Precision@K, NDCG@K)\n    \n- Easy to define strong DIY baselines\n    \n- Flexible: you can extend later (sequence, debiasing, cold start)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":800,
			"y":-140,
			"width":560,
			"height":260
		},
		{
			"id":"a8a6f604bd8b1805",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## 1️⃣ First: reduce the problem space (very important)\n\nYou were given **three canonical problems**:\n\n1. **Rating prediction**\n    \n2. **Next-item prediction**\n    \n3. **Top-N recommendation**\n    \n\nLet’s evaluate them **against your constraints**.\n\n### ❌ Rating prediction (I do NOT recommend this)\n\nWhy:\n\n- Requires **explicit feedback**\n    \n- Extremely sparse\n    \n- Less relevant in modern systems\n    \n- Weak alignment with real-world recommender pipelines\n    \n- Hard to incorporate exposure/position bias properly\n    \n\n**Verdict:**  \n❌ Avoid unless explicitly required\n\n---\n\n### ⚠️ Next-item prediction\n\nPros:\n\n- Very popular in research\n    \n- Clean sequential formulation\n    \n- Easy to use temporal splits\n    \n- Works well with implicit feedback\n    \n\nCons:\n\n- Often becomes _“train a Transformer and report NDCG”_\n    \n- Hard to show novelty **before midterms**\n    \n- Baselines are already strong\n    \n\n**Verdict:**  \n⚠️ Good, but risky unless you add a _clear modeling twist_\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Top-N recommendation (BEST choice)\n\nPros:\n\n- Directly matches **implicit feedback**\n    \n- Naturally incorporates:\n    \n    - Negative sampling\n        \n    - Temporal decay\n        \n    - Propensity weighting\n        \n- Evaluation metrics already aligned (Precision@K, NDCG@K)\n    \n- Easy to define strong DIY baselines\n    \n- Flexible: you can extend later (sequence, debiasing, cold start)\n    \n\n**Verdict:**  \n✅ **This should be your project framing**\n\n> 💡 Important insight:  \n> **Next-item prediction is just a special case of Top-N recommendation with temporal conditioning.**\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ Now let’s map your class pointers → a coherent project\n\nYou already have:\n\n- Implicit feedback\n    \n- Bias discussion\n    \n- Data leakage awareness\n    \n- Evaluation rigor\n    \n- Baselines\n    \n\nWhat’s missing?  \n👉 **A single clear research question**\n\n---\n\n## 3️⃣ Strong project framing (recommended)\n\n### 🎯 FINAL PROJECT CHOICE\n\n> **Top-N Recommendation from Implicit Feedback under Temporal and Exposure Bias**\n\nThis is:\n\n- Academically correct\n    \n- Industry relevant\n    \n- Feasible before midterms\n    \n- Extendable after midterms\n    \n\n---\n\n## 4️⃣ Convert this into a clean IEEE-style problem statement\n\n### **Problem Statement**\n\nUser preferences in modern recommender systems are primarily inferred from **implicit feedback** such as clicks and interactions. However, implicit feedback is a **noisy, biased proxy** of true user preference due to exposure bias, position bias, and missing negative signals. Traditional recommender models trained naively on such data often overestimate popular or highly exposed items and fail to generalize.\n\nThis project aims to design and evaluate a **Top-N recommendation framework** that:\n\n1. Learns user preferences from implicit feedback\n    \n2. Incorporates temporal dynamics of user behavior\n    \n3. Mitigates bias introduced by exposure and missing negatives\n    \n4. Evaluates recommendations using leakage-free temporal splits\n    \n\n---\n\n## 5️⃣ Literature survey (how to structure, not dump papers)\n\nYour survey should have **4 short subsections**, not a paper list.\n\n### 1. Implicit Feedback Recommendation\n\n- Difference between explicit vs implicit signals\n    \n- Why negative sampling is required\n    \n- Risks of treating unobserved as negative\n    \n\n### 2. Bias in Recommendation Systems\n\n- Exposure bias\n    \n- Position bias\n    \n- Confounding factors\n    \n- Why offline metrics can be misleading\n    \n\n### 3. Temporal Modeling of Preferences\n\n- User preferences evolve\n    \n- Temporal decay improves alignment\n    \n- Importance of event time vs log time\n    \n\n### 4. Evaluation Challenges\n\n- Offline vs online evaluation\n    \n- Temporal splits\n    \n- Leakage traps\n    \n\n> ⚠️ Keep this **conceptual**, not citation-heavy.  \n> IEEE reviewers like clarity more than name-dropping.\n\n---\n\n## 6️⃣ Proposed method (simple, correct, research-grade)\n\nThis is where many students overcomplicate. Don’t.\n\n### **Data formulation**\n\n- Implicit feedback: (user, item, timestamp, event_type)\n    \n- Positive samples = observed interactions\n    \n- Negative samples = unobserved items (sampled)\n    \n\n### **Model pipeline**\n\n1. **User representation**\n    \n    - Aggregation of interacted item embeddings\n        \n    - Temporal decay applied to older interactions\n        \n2. **Item representation**\n    \n    - Learnable embeddings\n        \n3. **Scoring function**\n    \n    - Dot product or shallow neural layer\n        \n4. **Bias handling**\n    \n    - Propensity weighting OR\n        \n    - Exposure-aware negative sampling\n        \n5. **Training objective**\n    \n    - Pairwise ranking loss (BPR-style)\n        \n    - Weighted by temporal decay\n        \n\n---\n\n## 7️⃣ Baselines (VERY important for marks)\n\nYou already listed the perfect ones:\n\n- Popularity-based\n    \n- Recency-based\n    \n- Co-visitation-based\n    \n- Random\n    \n\n> 🚨 Explicitly say:  \n> “All baselines are implemented from scratch to ensure fair comparison.”\n\n---\n\n## 8️⃣ Evaluation (aligns perfectly with class notes)\n\n### Offline evaluation\n\n- Temporal split (NO random split)\n    \n- Per-user evaluation\n    \n- Metrics:\n    \n    - Precision@K\n        \n    - Recall@K\n        \n    - NDCG@K\n        \n\n### Sanity checks\n\n- Popular items vs long-tail\n    \n- Effect of temporal decay\n    \n- Ablation: with/without bias correction\n    \n\n---\n\n## 9️⃣ Cold start (address, don’t solve fully)\n\nBefore midterms, **acknowledge**, don’t fully solve:\n\n- New users → fallback to popularity/recency\n    \n- New items → exposure-based warm-up\n    \n- State clearly as _future work_\n    \n\nThis shows maturity.\n\n---\n\n## 🔟 Final decision summary (what you should submit)\n\n✅ **Chosen problem**: Top-N recommendation  \n✅ **Feedback type**: Implicit  \n✅ **Core challenges addressed**:\n\n- Bias\n    \n- Temporal dynamics\n    \n- Leakage-free evaluation  \n    ✅ **Extendable later** to:\n    \n- Sequential models\n    \n- Contrastive learning\n    \n- Cold start solutions\n    \n\n---\n\n## If you want next:\n\nI can:\n\n- Convert this **directly into IEEE conference template text**\n    \n- Write **Problem Statement + Literature Survey sections verbatim**\n    \n- Design **figure diagrams** (data flow, evaluation split)\n    \n- Help you choose **dataset (MovieLens vs Amazon vs synthetic)**\n    \n\nJust tell me:  \n👉 _“Write the IEEE report draft”_ or _“Help me extend novelty”_",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1400,
			"y":-100,
			"width":540,
			"height":2040
		},
		{
			"id":"7154256af3786c5a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Recent Trends\n\nDeep learning recommender systems have rapidly evolved in the last few years. Modern **transformer-based models and large language models (LLMs)** are now widely applied to recommendation. For example, sequential recommendation models such as SASRec and BERT4Rec use self-attention to capture long-term user-item dynamics. Even general LLMs (e.g. ChatGPT) have been integrated: systems like _Chat-Rec_ employ in-context learning with a pretrained LLM to conduct conversational recommendations and cross-domain suggestions, and other studies (e.g. BookGPT) use LLMs to generate item summaries and ratings. This reflects the trend of leveraging pretrained NLP models and prompt engineering to enrich recommendation tasks.\n\nAnother major trend is **graph neural networks (GNNs)**. In recommendation, users and items naturally form graphs, and recent work shows GNNs “efficiently leverage complex, relational user-item interaction data, enhancing recommendation accuracy and personalization”. Modern GNN-based recommenders (e.g. LightGCN, NGCF, KGAT) propagate features on user–item graphs and knowledge graphs to improve link prediction and diversity. Emerging variants fuse multi-modal data and address scalability – for example, Hypergraph or Heterogeneous GNNs incorporate rich context like multimodal features or side information.\n\nA third trend is **self-supervised and contrastive learning**. Data sparsity and cold-start drive methods that generate auxiliary tasks without labels. In particular, _contrastive learning_ has “emerged as a prominent self-supervised approach” in recommendation. Recent papers propose diverse data augmentations (e.g. drop edges in interaction graphs or mask sequence items) and maximize agreement between augmented views. These approaches improve robustness to noise and sparsity. Likewise, **reconstructive pretraining** (e.g. masked autoencoders) and **generative diffusion models** are being adopted: very recent work uses denoising diffusion to generate or augment user interaction data. In fact, diffusion-based methods have “emerged as a new trend in SSL-based recommendation”.\n\nSequential and reinforcement learning (RL) approaches also remain active. RL-based recommenders (e.g. bandit or policy-gradient agents) model the long-term user engagement, but they require careful design due to non-stationarity. Surveys note ongoing interest in RL for user modeling and in merging RL with deep sequential models.\n\nFinally, there is growing emphasis on **trustworthy and responsible recommender systems**. Explainability and interpretability are viewed as critical: recent work integrates explainability modules or causal analysis into deep models to justify recommendations. Fairness and transparency are top concerns: since 2020 there has been a clear shift toward _fairness-aware_ and _transparency-focused_ recommendation methods. For example, surveys highlight the importance of modeling user- and item-level fairness and diversity. Privacy preservation is also key: federated learning and differential privacy techniques are actively developed so that training can occur on user devices without leaking personal data. In summary, contemporary research combines powerful deep architectures (transformers, GNNs, LLMs) with self-supervised learning and new safeguards for fairness, explainability, and privacy.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1980,
			"y":-100,
			"width":800,
			"height":1000
		},
		{
			"id":"c7a44241ccf93fef",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Project Idea 1: Theoretical Focus\n\n**Fairness-Constrained Graph-Contrastive Recommender:** Develop a novel algorithmic model that blends graph neural networks, contrastive self-supervision, and fairness constraints. Concretely, we propose a GNN-based recommender on the user–item interaction graph that is trained with a contrastive loss (augmenting the graph or sequences) to learn robust embeddings. On top of this, we introduce a theoretical fairness regularizer (e.g. demographic parity or equity constraints) into the loss. The project will derive and implement the new training objective and architecture, and **theoretically analyze** the accuracy–fairness tradeoff. For example, one can prove bounds on how the fairness regularizer affects generalization or convergence. This idea is timely because fairness-aware learning is a highlighted trend, and it combines recent advances in GNNs and contrastive SSL. Implementation would involve coding a deep model (e.g. using PyTorch/Deep Graph Library), creating or using graph augmentations for contrastive learning, and incorporating fairness constraints (perhaps via Lagrange multipliers or adversarial debiasing). The project is research-level: it involves novel model/algorithm design and mathematical analysis, while also requiring extensive experiments on benchmark data (e.g. MovieLens or Gowalla) to validate that the new model improves fairness without sacrificing accuracy.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1980,
			"y":940,
			"width":800,
			"height":440,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"c1a7508887ebcf9b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Project Idea 2: Applied Focus\n\n**LLM-Augmented Multimodal Recommendation:** Build and evaluate a recommender system that leverages large language models to enrich item and user features. In a practical setting (e.g. an e-commerce or movie dataset with reviews and descriptions), we will use a pretrained LLM (e.g. GPT-3.5 or LLaMA) to **generate or encode rich content** for items and possibly users. For instance, we can prompt an LLM to summarize item attributes or user preferences based on their reviews. These LLM-derived embeddings (or generated descriptions) are then fused with traditional collaborative features (user/item IDs) in a deep recommendation model. As one concrete strategy (inspired by recent work), we might generate item descriptions via few-shot prompting and then encode them (e.g. with a BERT encoder) alongside item ID embeddings. We would train a hybrid model (e.g. a transformer or multi-layer neural CF model) on real interaction data, comparing performance with and without the LLM features. This applied project will involve dataset collection (e.g. Amazon product reviews, Yelp or MovieLens with text), implementing LLM calls, training neural models, and evaluating metrics like NDCG or recall. The novelty comes from leveraging cutting-edge LLMs in a recommender pipeline and experimenting on real data. This idea is timely given industry interest in AI-driven recommendation and explainability, and recent studies show that LLM-augmented features can meaningfully improve recommendation quality. A successful implementation could yield a powerful, content-enriched RS and empirical insights on the gains from LLM knowledge in practice.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1980,
			"y":1420,
			"width":800,
			"height":520,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"150efef4adc1b5ea",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# A. Graph Neural Networks for Recommendation (Base Architecture)\n\nThese define the **backbone** of your model.\n\n---\n\n### 1. _Neural Graph Collaborative Filtering_ (NGCF)\n\n**Wang et al., SIGIR 2019**  \nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/1905.08108\n\n**Why relevant**\n\n- One of the first GNN-based recommenders\n    \n- Explicitly models user–item bipartite graph\n    \n- Strong baseline for graph-based CF\n    \n\nYou can say:\n\n> “We adopt a graph-based message passing framework similar to NGCF…”\n\n---\n\n### 2. _LightGCN: Simplifying and Powering GCN for Recommendation_\n\n**He et al., SIGIR 2020**  \nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2002.02126\n\n**Why relevant**\n\n- Removes nonlinearities → shows **what really matters**\n    \n- Very strong baseline, widely used\n    \n- Ideal **starting backbone** before adding contrastive + fairness losses\n    \n\nYou’ll almost certainly **compare against LightGCN**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. _PinSage: A Graph Convolutional Neural Network for Web-Scale Recommender Systems_\n\n**Ying et al., KDD 2018 (Pinterest)**  \nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/1806.01973\n\n**Why relevant**\n\n- Industrial-scale motivation\n    \n- Shows graph sampling + embedding propagation\n    \n- Good citation to justify **graph formulation**",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2960,
			"y":-80,
			"width":540,
			"height":1340
		},
		{
			"id":"001d4bd77852c43b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# B. Contrastive / Self-Supervised Learning in Recommendation\n\nThis supports your **contrastive objective**.\n\n---\n\n### 4. _Self-Supervised Graph Learning for Recommendation_\n\n**Wu et al., SIGIR 2021**  \nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2010.10783\n\n**Why relevant**\n\n- Introduces contrastive learning on interaction graphs\n    \n- Uses graph augmentations (edge dropping, node masking)\n    \n- Very close to your “graph-contrastive recommender” idea\n    \n\nThis is a **must-cite** paper.\n\n---\n\n### 5. _Contrastive Learning for Recommender Systems: A Survey_\n\n**Zhou et al., ACM Computing Surveys 2023**  \nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2301.01343\n\n**Why relevant**\n\n- Lets you justify contrastive SSL cleanly\n    \n- Helps you position your method among existing SSL techniques\n    \n- Excellent for literature survey section\n    \n\n---\n\n### 6. _CL4Rec: Contrastive Learning for Recommendation_\n\n**Yu et al., WWW 2022**  \nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2204.08587\n\n**Why relevant**\n\n- Demonstrates performance gains from contrastive views\n    \n- Focuses on robustness and sparsity\n    \n- You can adapt similar objectives to **graph views**",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":3520,
			"y":-80,
			"width":540,
			"height":1340
		},
		{
			"id":"aab8a64ca5747e15",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# C. Fairness in Recommendation Systems (Critical for Novelty)\n\nThis is where **your main novelty lives**.\n\n---\n\n### 7. _Fairness-Aware Recommendation: An Overview_\n\n**Burke et al., ACM TiiS 2017**  \nhttps://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3052569\n\n**Why relevant**\n\n- Classic taxonomy: user fairness, item fairness, exposure fairness\n    \n- Helps define _what fairness means_ in RS\n    \n\nGood for **problem motivation**.\n\n---\n\n### 8. _Causal Fairness in Recommender Systems_\n\n**Zhang et al., WWW 2021**  \nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2102.12529\n\n**Why relevant**\n\n- Highlights bias sources (exposure, popularity)\n    \n- Motivates fairness constraints beyond accuracy\n    \n\nYou don’t need causal modeling, but the **bias argument** is strong.\n\n---\n\n### 9. _Fairness-Aware Graph Neural Networks_\n\n**Dai & Wang, KDD 2021**  \nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2106.06166\n\n**Why relevant**\n\n- Shows how fairness constraints can be injected into GNNs\n    \n- Not recommendation-specific → **gap you can exploit**\n    \n- Useful for designing your fairness regularizer\n    \n\n---\n\n### 10. _Debiasing Learning for Fair Recommendation_\n\n**Wu et al., NeurIPS 2021**  \nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2106.03570\n\n**Why relevant**\n\n- Uses reweighting / regularization to reduce bias\n    \n- Demonstrates trade-off between accuracy and fairness\n    \n- Good reference when discussing **accuracy–fairness tradeoff**\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":4080,
			"y":-80,
			"width":540,
			"height":1340
		},
		{
			"id":"c2b34e63eb0a87ee",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# D. Accuracy–Fairness Trade-off & Theory\n\nThis helps you justify the **theoretical analysis** part.\n\n---\n\n### 11. _On the Trade-off Between Accuracy and Fairness in Recommendation Systems_\n\n**Ekstrand et al., RecSys 2018**  \nhttps://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3240323.3240374\n\n**Why relevant**\n\n- Empirical evidence that fairness constraints affect accuracy\n    \n- Sets up motivation for your theoretical analysis\n    \n\n---\n\n### 12. _Generalization Bounds for Fair Learning_\n\n**Donini et al., NeurIPS 2018**  \nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/1808.06613\n\n**Why relevant**\n\n- Provides **theoretical tools**\n    \n- You can cite this when you say:\n    \n    > “We analyze how fairness regularization impacts generalization…”\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":4640,
			"y":-80,
			"width":540,
			"height":1340
		},
		{
			"id":"d95213f60852522e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# How to Position Your Novelty (VERY IMPORTANT)\n\nAfter citing these, you can clearly say:\n\n> Existing graph-based recommenders optimize accuracy alone.  \n> Existing contrastive recommenders improve robustness but ignore fairness.  \n> Existing fairness-aware models rarely combine **graph structure + self-supervision**.\n\n### Your gap:\n\n**No unified framework that jointly:**\n\n- learns graph-based representations\n    \n- uses contrastive self-supervision\n    \n- enforces fairness constraints\n ",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2940,
			"y":1320,
			"width":340,
			"height":620
		},
		{
			"id":"4a58f8d2fb9b2908",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Minimal Citation Set (If you want to keep it lean)\n\nIf your instructor prefers fewer citations, **use these 7**:\n\n1. NGCF (graph rec)\n    \n2. LightGCN (baseline)\n    \n3. Wu et al. 2021 (graph SSL)\n    \n4. Contrastive RS survey (2023)\n    \n5. Burke et al. (fairness overview)\n    \n6. Fairness-aware GNN (KDD 2021)\n    \n7. Ekstrand et al. (accuracy–fairness tradeoff)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":3320,
			"y":1320,
			"width":340,
			"height":620
		},
		{
			"id":"a5f881a9dfac8fc1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## ⭐ Highly Relevant Papers For Your Project\n\nIf you want _novelty and direct inspiration_ for your _fairness-constrained graph-contrastive model_, these are good ones to build on:\n\n### 🔹 Graph + Fairness\n\n1. **Heterophily-Aware Fair Recommendation using Graph Convolutional Networks**  \n    _Gholinejad et al., 2024_ — addresses fairness in GNN based recommenders by adjusting aggregation/normalization to reduce bias.  \n    https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.03365\n    \n2. **Learning Fair Representations for Recommendation: A Graph-Based Perspective**  \n    _Wu et al., 2021_ — learns graph embeddings that obfuscate sensitive attributes (adversarial fairness).  \n    https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.09140\n    \n\nBoth are _closer to fairness in graph contexts_ than your IEEE reference.\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 Contrastive Learning in Graph Recommendation\n\n3. **LightGCL: Graph Contrastive Learning for Recommendation**  \n    _Cai et al., 2023_ — simple but effective CL framework for graph-based recommendation, focusing on data augmentation limitations.  \n    https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.08191\n    \n\nThis is exactly the type of contrastive RS model you want to build upon.\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 Fairness in Sequential / Graph Recommendation\n\n4. **FairSR: Fairness-aware Sequential Recommendation with Preference Graph Embeddings**  \n    _Li et al., 2022_ — combines fairness constraints with sequential recommendation and graph modeling.  \n    https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.00313\n    \n\nThis is a _fairness + graph embedding_ work that is _closest to your direction_.",
